Description: Loops that stay in time, and a loop section that cannot latch
 1. Loop quantise always takes the *nearest* beat, passed or not. Mixxx
    snapped loop-in and loop-out to the closest beat only while no loop was
    running; once a loop was active -- which is the state the press half of
    every IN/OUT tap puts the deck in, via m_bAdjustingLoopIn/Out -- it
    snapped loop-in to the previous beat and loop-out to the *next* one. A
    tap a few milliseconds after the beat therefore bought a loop one whole
    beat too long, and the deck dropped out of time. Both ends now take the
    nearest beat and only fall back to the beat on the far side when the
    nearest one would invert the loop (or run off the end of the track),
    which LoopingControl answers by discarding the loop. Snapping to a beat
    that has just gone by does not cost the mix its timing: the play position
    is carried into the new loop in whole loop lengths by
    adjustedPositionInsideAdjustedLoop(), so it keeps its place in the bar.
 2. DDJ-400 mapping, SHIFT + LOOP IN / LOOP OUT: the guard read
    engine.getValue(group, "loop_enabled" === 0), i.e. it asked the deck for
    a control named "false" and got 0 back, so it never blocked anything.
    The jog-wheel loop adjust could be armed on a deck with no loop, and
    nothing clears the flag until a loop has been enabled and disabled again.
    While it is set jogTouch() returns early, so the platter stops scratching
    altogether, and as soon as a loop does appear the wheel drags the loop in
    point instead of nudging the track. The guard now reads the control, the
    flags are cleared when a deck loads a track, and a jog-wheel adjust is
    clamped so the two loop points can never meet or cross (which makes
    LoopingControl throw the loop away mid-adjust) and the out point cannot
    be pushed past the end of the track.
 3. The DDJ-400 mapping switches QUANTIZE on for both decks at startup, like
    a Pioneer player boots. Mixxx defaults it off per deck and then remembers
    it, so a rig could sit with one deck quantised and the other not. The
    on-screen QUANTIZE button still turns it off for the rest of the session.
